TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2) – Space Mission Design","overview":"Students design a preliminary space mission benefiting Australia, covering the space segment: mission purpose, spacecraft count, users, launch site and vehicle, orbit and orbital elements, manoeuvres, ground control and communications, mission duration and end-of-life. Design may use STK, PIGI or approved software, supplemented by hand calculations, and is reported as a concise technical report with supporting screenshots and software files."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale - Include the following required information: • What is the spacecraft's primary mission? • Why is the mission needed? • How many spacecraft are used and why? • Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design - Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate - Include relevant screenshots and software files (in the main body of the report and/or appendix) - Include the following information: • Where it will launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? • What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? • Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? • Which ground control facilities will you use? • How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? • How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (eg. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"partial","where":"Mission Design"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"yes","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R11","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R12","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R13","met":"partial","where":"Executive Summary"},{"id":"R14","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R15","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The reported 600 km, 97.79° near-circular orbit is broadly plausible for a sun-synchronous orbit, but a specific local solar time of the ascending/descending node is not given. Therefore the assertion that the descending track is constantly sunlit and the claimed illumination conditions are not established."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"RAAN is not determined by the launch facility's location. The statement that the initial RAAN indicates the launch facility is incorrect; injection geometry, launch epoch, and the selected orbital plane determine the initial RAAN."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"The report selects Vega E while acknowledging it had not been built. This is not a demonstrated available launch option; rideshare eligibility, deployment orbit compatibility, schedule, and capability are not substantiated."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The report gives no sensor specification, ground sample distance, spectral capability, swath, or underwater-performance analysis to show that a satellite at 600 km can provide useful fluid-lensing reef imagery or the stated monitoring products."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The access table contains a duplicated access entry and reports a maximum contact of about 12.8 minutes, not an average of almost ten minutes. It does not establish data volume or link-budget adequacy."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"A near-polar orbit does not by itself ensure long contact for the vast majority of passes; contact depends on station latitude, elevation mask, orbit geometry, and ground-station antenna constraints."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"The report describes an EOL deorbit manoeuvre but also states the satellite has no thrust capability. A drag sail is a passive disposal device, not an orbital manoeuvre; no deployment design or decay-time estimate is provided."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"A five-year useful lifespan is asserted without supporting spacecraft reliability, power, or consumables analysis. The text also suggests solar panels may lengthen life without explaining the limiting-life mechanism."},{"where":"Bibliography","issue":"Reference [6] is Wikipedia, which the assignment explicitly disallows as a proper source. Citations numbered [49]–[74] in the launch tables do not have corresponding bibliography entries."}],"overall":"A coherent Australian reef-monitoring concept is presented, with useful STK material and coverage of most required mission sections. The strongest limitations are unverified payload feasibility, flawed orbital and EOL reasoning, speculative launch selection, and incomplete citation and figure/table presentation.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":7.0,"scale":10.0,"points":7.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Clearly explains the reef-monitoring mission, its Australian environmental rationale, the one-spacecraft choice and intended users. The operator is only described broadly as Australian facilities, and user roles and operational responsibilities are not developed in detail; this supports the lower end of Accomplished.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"The satellite, nicknamed ZYZZsat (Z-axis/Y-axis Zeroed Zoning Satellite), is operated and accessed from Australia ground facilities"},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required. This also just allows for simplicity in communications, reduces cost, and requires less time to build and launch."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM) for research and/or education."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"Covers launch, orbital elements, STK access results, ground station, duration and EOL, with useful screenshots and calculations. However, the launcher choice is speculative, orbital lighting and RAAN claims are weak or incorrect, payload performance is not quantified, and EOL manoeuvre capability is contradictory. Software files and several design assumptions are not supplied or validated.","evidence":[{"where":"Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle – launched from the Guiana Space Centre Spaceport."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"Specifically, an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of $ 97.7924^{\\circ} $ for the satellite to remain sun-synchronous."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"An average contact duration of almost ten minutes was achieved, in addition to the already frequent amount of passes."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"The most promising alternative is a ‘drag sail’ – a drag-enhancing deorbit device that deploys at the EOL."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion recaps the selected mission configuration and acknowledges launch uncertainty, but mostly restates design choices. It does not draw sufficiently detailed conclusions from the reported access results, orbital analysis, or unresolved feasibility issues.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A polar sun-synchronous orbit allows for consistent illumination of the oceans while maintaining a constant sun angle."},{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"Thus, launch may need to be adjusted if the rocket is still not constructed by the proposed date."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report has a recognisable structure, executive summary, headings, and generally understandable prose. Grammar and wording errors remain, figures and tables are not consistently numbered and fully captioned, and references are bare URLs with inconsistent citation numbering; Wikipedia is used as a source despite the task instructions.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"This report outlines the intended designs and procedures involved in the construction of a Earth observation satellite"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[6] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"DN path for orbits 1-15"}]}]}
